Myth 6: You Should Drink Less Water During Your Period

There is no need to avoid water simply because you are menstruating. Staying hydrated is an important part of general health.

Myth 7: Talking About Periods Is Embarrassing

Menstruation is a normal biological process. Talking openly about periods can help girls and women get accurate information and access the menstrual products they need.

Parents, teachers, partners, and healthcare professionals can all play an important role in creating a more supportive environment.

Myth 8: A Late Period Always Means Something Is Wrong

Periods can occasionally arrive earlier or later than expected. Stress, illness, hormonal changes, lifestyle changes, and other factors can affect menstrual timing.

However, persistent irregularity or significant changes should be discussed with a healthcare professional.

Final Thoughts

Understanding the facts about menstruation can help reduce unnecessary fear and embarrassment. Periods are a normal part of life, and everyone deserves access to accurate information and suitable menstrual products.
